From 2e62be3b54cc5ff56b52d4b8f3e60cda6e018b82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian MacDonald <ian@netstatz.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 17:55:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 74/88] docs(config): WebSocket connections now count against
 the per-client cap

Assisted-by: claude:opus-5.5
---
 docs/guide/configuration/README.md | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/docs/guide/configuration/README.md b/docs/guide/configuration/README.md
index 6ecd8637d..8022401f2 100644
--- a/docs/guide/configuration/README.md
+++ b/docs/guide/configuration/README.md
@@ -230,7 +230,7 @@ When `lemond` starts, effective configuration is resolved by deep-merging settin
 | `max_loaded_models` | int | 1 | Max models per type slot. Use -1 for unlimited |
 | `max_request_body_mb` | int | 100 | Largest request body the server accepts, in MB (1–4096), including chunked uploads. A larger body is refused with 413. The bundled HTTP library already defaulted to 100 MB; builds against an older system cpp-httplib had no limit before. On a memory-constrained gateway set it lower, for example `32`: that still fits a 25 MB audio upload. Changing it restarts the HTTP listeners. |
 | `request_receive_timeout` | int | 0 | Seconds a client has to send a complete request, headers and body, counted from when a server thread starts serving the connection (0–3600). `0` turns the limit off. The read timeout alone only limits the gap between two reads, so a client that sends a byte every few seconds can otherwise hold a worker thread indefinitely. While it is set, each connection carries one request (no keep-alive reuse). Connections upgraded to WebSocket on the main port are not covered. Not enforced on Windows. Changing it restarts the HTTP listeners. |
-| `max_connections_per_client` | int | 0 | Most connections one client address may have open at once (0–1024). `0` means no limit. Further connections are refused with 503. Loopback clients are exempt, and connections upgraded to WebSocket stop counting once upgraded. The cap bounds what one address can hold, but several addresses together can still hold every worker with silent connections, so on a gateway set `request_receive_timeout` as well. Changing it restarts the HTTP listeners. |
+| `max_connections_per_client` | int | 0 | Most connections one client address may have open at once (0–1024). `0` means no limit. Further connections are refused with 503. Loopback clients are exempt. WebSocket connections, on the main port or the WebSocket port, are counted against the same cap separately from HTTP connections, and at most 64 WebSocket connections are open in total. The cap bounds what one address can hold, but several addresses together can still hold every worker with silent connections, so on a gateway set `request_receive_timeout` as well. Changing it restarts the HTTP listeners. |
 | `auto_evict` | bool | false | Enable dynamic VRAM management based on idle time and global GPU memory pressure. Can be overridden per model. |
 | `auto_evict_threshold_pct` | number | 0.90 | Global VRAM fraction at which pressure eviction is evaluated. Must be greater than 0 and at most 1.0; `0.90` means 90%. |
 | `beacon_listen` | bool | false | Linux/macOS only. Listen on UDP 13305 for other Lemonade servers' LAN broadcast beacons from on-link RFC1918 senders and list them under `beacon_listener` in `/v1/health`. Heard hosts are never contacted and never used as providers. The list is as readable as `/health`, so set `LEMONADE_API_KEY` when `host` is not localhost. `stats.url_mismatch` counts multi-homed peers whose source IP differs from their advertised URL, and beacons re-sent by a relay that is not listed in `beacon_trusted_relays`. UDP 13305 must be allowed inbound on the LAN side. By default it hears every RFC1918 interface, so on a gateway whose WAN side is also RFC1918 (double NAT), set `beacon_listen_interfaces` to the LAN bridges, or WAN-side hosts are listed too. |
